.TH TIFILEUTIL 1 "August 1, 2023"

.SH NAME
tifileutil - a program for manipulating computer files suitable for TI graphing calculators

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtifileutil\fP [\-h / \-\-help] [\-v / \-\-version] [command] [arguments]

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBtifileutil\fP program allows you to perform various operations on computer files suitable for the TI-Z80, TI-eZ80 and TI-68k series of graphing calculators.

.SH OPTIONS
\fBtifileutil\fP accepts the following options:

.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fP
Display a list of all command line options and commands.

.TP
\fB\-v, \-\-version\fP
Output the version info.

.SH COMMANDS
\fBtifileutil\fP accepts the following commands:

.TP
.B wrap [OPTIONS] [FILE | \-]
Wrap raw data into a regular TI variable file, adding the computer-side header,
footer and checksum.
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fB\-o OUTFILE\fP: output result to OUTFILE
.IP \(bu
\fB\-n NAME\fP: set on-calc variable name to NAME (TI-Z80, TI-eZ80, TI-68k)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-f NAME\fP: set on-calc variable folder to NAME (TI-68k)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-t TYPE\fP: set variable type to TYPE (e.g. 82p)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-V VERSION\fP: set file version to VERSION (TI-Z80, TI-eZ80)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-c COMMENT\fP: set file comment to COMMENT (strftime format string)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-\-raw\-comment=COMMENT\fP: set file comment to COMMENT literally (no strftime
expansion; when both are given, the last of \fB\-c\fP and \fB\-\-raw\-comment\fP wins)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-x EXTENSION\fP: set 1-4 chars "extension" for "other" variable type (TI-68k)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-p\fP: protect program (TI-Z80, TI-eZ80)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-C\fP: number/list/matrix is complex (TI-Z80, TI-eZ80; exact fractions,
radicals and pi on TI-83PCE/TI-84PCE)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-P\fP: pack data as a TI-83PCE/TI-84+CE Python AppVar (with \fB\-t 8xv\fP;
the 8xv extension maps to the TI-83+ model, which is accepted as well):
the data is stored as a legacy Python AppVar payload (\fBPYCD\fP magic), which
TI-Connect and the TI-83PCE/TI-84+CE can run
.PP
When wrapping TI-68k string, assembly, text, picture and "other" variables, the
2-byte size field and the per-type structure are added automatically (TI-68k
strings may contain 0x00 bytes, as supported by some old tools; assembly
programs are emitted as [2-byte size][code][relocation data][0xF3]; text line
endings, be they CR, LF or CR+LF, map to CR; picture dimensions are synthesized
from the input size, up to 240x103 pixels, i.e. 3090 bytes).
.IP \(bu
\fB\-a\fP: mark file for sending to archive (TI-Z80, TI-eZ80, TI-68k)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-l\fP: mark file locked (TI-68k)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-r\fP: raw mode (no length bytes)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-v\fP: be verbose
.RE

.TP
.B unwrap [OPTIONS] FILE
Strip the computer-side header and trailing checksum of a regular TI file, in
order to extract the raw binary content of the variable.
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fB\-o OUTFILE\fP: output result to OUTFILE (\fB\-\fP or absent: stdout)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-d\fP: deep unwrapping (beyond the computer-side header and footer): for
TI-83+/84+/CSE/CE protected programs, strip the tokenized name prefix; for
TI-68k files, strip the 2-byte size field and the per-type envelope (the
relocation-size field and 0xF3 tag of assembly programs, the leading 0x00 and
STR_TAG of string variables, the extension and OTH_TAG of other data, the
cursor position, line types and TEXT_TAG of text variables, which are output
with CR line endings, or the height, width and PIC_TAG of pictures);
for Python AppVars, extract the script from the \fBPYCD\fP / \fBPYSC\fP payload
.IP \(bu
\fB\-v\fP: be verbose
.RE
.PP
TI flash (OS and app) files have no header or trailing checksum to strip, so
they are passed through unchanged.

.TP
.B squish [OPTIONS] FILE
Convert the hexadecimal text stored inside a TI-Z80 (TI-73/82/83/83+/84+/CSE/CE)
program variable into binary code, producing a protected program (type 0x06)
whose data carries the 2-byte length field and the model-specific
squished-program marker (none on TI-73/83, D5 00 11 on TI-82, BB 6D on
TI-83+/84+, EF 69 on TI-84+CSE, EF 7B on TI-83PCE/TI-84PCE), as used by shells
such as SOS, ASHELL and ZASMLOAD. On TI-82, the marker is followed by the
shell's long name, NUL-terminated; on TI-83, the shell headers carry a
description. On TI-73, the Mallard header (D9 00, the 4-character program
name "Duck", the entry point at userMem + header length and the NUL-terminated
description) or the shell-less nostub header (prgm0000, '1', the code size in
decimal and a hard newline, run by the built-in assembly support of OS 1.40+,
limited to 900 bytes of code) can be emitted.
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fB\-o OUTFILE\fP: output result to OUTFILE (required)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-s SHELL\fP: target shell - TI-82: crash (default, D5 00 11), ace (D5 00 A0),
ash (D9 00 30) or sng ("SNG"); TI-73: mallard (D9 00 "Duck" + entry point +
description) or nostub (prgm0000 + '1' + decimal size + code, for OS 1.40+);
TI-83: ion ([C9][30 len] + description), ashell ([00][18 XX] with a title
pointer), venus (the "E7 39 5F 5B 56 3F 00" magic), ion83, pse83, ice or sos
(the tokenized "Send(9prgm<name>" magics of Ion v1.6, PSE, Ice and SOS, with
the fixed shell names IONZ, PSEZ, ZICE and ZSOSL, followed by "3F D9 3F 2A"
(line separator, "Stop", line separator, quote) for ion83/pse83 or by
"3F E1" (line separator, "ClLCD") for ice/sos); TI-83+/84+:
ion (the marker and the ION header), ion83, pse83 or ice (the tokenized
"Asm prgm<name>" variants of the same magics), tse ([C9]["TSE"][01] + a
space-prefixed title + the 2-byte external data size) or mirage ([C9][01] +
a 30-byte icon + a description); the default on TI-73/83 is the shell-less
ZASMLOAD form
.IP \(bu
\fB\-L NAME\fP: set the shell program description or TSE/MirageOS title
(default: the variable name; not usable with the nostub, ion83, pse83, ice
and sos formats, whose headers carry no description)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-v\fP: be verbose
.RE

.TP
.B unsquish [OPTIONS] FILE
Convert binary code stored inside a TI-Z80 program variable into hexadecimal
text, producing an unprotected program (type 0x05) whose data carries the
2-byte length field and which can be run on shell-less TI-73/83 calculators
(whose hex text is appended the "End 0000 End" trailer). On TI-82, the
shell-specific marker (D5 00 11, D5 00 A0, D9 00 30 or "SNG") and the
shell's long name are stripped; on TI-73/83/83+/84+, the ION header (marker,
description and title) is stripped, on TI-73 additionally the Mallard header
(D9 00, name, entry point and description) and the nostub header (prgm0000,
type, size and hard newline, the hex text of an unsquished nostub program
being passed through), and on TI-83 also the ASHELL83 and Venus headers, the
"Send(9prgm<name>" headers of the Ion v1.6, PSE, Ice and SOS shells, the
[C9][02] Ion module prefix and the [00 01] SOS module prefix. On TI-83+/84+,
the "Asm prgm<name>" headers of the Ion v1.6, PSE and Ice shells, the TSE
header (["TSE"][01], title and external data size), the MirageOS headers
([C9][01][icon] + description, the modified [C9][03][icon][quit address] +
description, and the [C9][02] external interface, whose prefix is also the
Ion module prefix) and the [C9][02] Ion module prefix are stripped as well.
As a special case, a TI-68k
assembly program (type 0x21) is turned into a TI-BASIC Exec string, in the
manner of ttasm2exec.pl.
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fB\-o OUTFILE\fP: output result to OUTFILE (\fB\-\fP or absent: stdout; required
for TI-Z80 files)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-v\fP: be verbose
.RE

.TP
.B checksum [OPTIONS] FILE
Check the checksum of a regular TI file, and optionally recompute and fix it.
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fB\-f\fP: recompute the stored checksum and write a corrected file (requires \fB\-o\fP)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-o OUTFILE\fP: output corrected file to OUTFILE (may equal the input file)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-v\fP: be verbose
.RE
.PP
The computed checksum and the checksum embedded into the file are printed. If
they don't match, a message is printed on stderr and the exit status is 2.

.TP
.B metadata -m MODE [OPTIONS] [FILE | -]
Print, retrieve, set or apply metadata of a file under a parsable
"\fBkey\fP": "\fBvalue\fP" form, with one pair per line. The MODE is one of
\fBgather\fP, \fBapply\fP, \fBget\fP and \fBset\fP.
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fBgather\fP: print the metadata of the input file to the output file
(absent: stdout)
.IP \(bu
\fBapply\fP: apply the metadata read from the \fB\-i FILE\fP input file
(absent: stdin) to the input TI file, writing the result to \fB\-o OUTFILE\fP
if given, and rewriting the input file otherwise. The input stream must start
with the "\fBmetadata.version\fP": "1" pair emitted by gather; unknown,
read-only and duplicate keys are errors.
.IP \(bu
\fBget\fP: print the value of the metadata requested by a single option
.IP \(bu
\fBset\fP: set one or more metadata fields on the input TI file, writing the
result to \fB\-o OUTFILE\fP if given, and rewriting the input file otherwise
.RE
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fB\-i FILE\fP: for apply, read metadata from FILE (absent: stdin)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-o OUTFILE\fP: output result to OUTFILE (gather: absent means stdout;
apply and set: absent means rewriting the input file)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-v\fP: be verbose
.RE
.PP
Single, group, backup, flash and tigroup files are supported. The file is
classified by its content (magic number and type byte), the extension being
only cross-checked, so that files with a missing, unrecognized or wrong
extension are still parsed. The emitted pairs use the following keys, all
values being strings:
.PP
.RS
.nf
metadata.version           version of the interchange format (always "1")
file.class                 single, group, backup, flash or tigroup
file.model                 calculator model (e.g. "TI83+")
file.magic                 8 first bytes of the file (4 for ZIP-based tigroup files)
file.comment               file comment (regular, backup, tigroup)
file.name                  application or OS name (flash)
file.checksum.computed     computed data checksum (regular, backup)
file.checksum.stored       stored data checksum (regular, backup)
file.entries               number of entries (1 for single files)
file.warnings              space-separated list of warnings, when any:
                           "checksum" (stored and computed checksums differ),
                           "extension_mismatch" (extension and content disagree)
entry[N].name             on-calc variable name
entry[N].folder           on-calc variable folder (TI-68k)
entry[N].type             variable type as an integer
entry[N].type.name        variable type name (e.g. "PRGM")
entry[N].type.ext         variable type extension (e.g. "8xp")
entry[N].size             variable data size in bytes
entry[N].version          file version (TI-Z80, TI-eZ80)
entry[N].attr.archived    1 if archived, 0 otherwise
entry[N].attr.locked      1 if locked, 0 otherwise
.fi
.RE
.PP
Tigroup files additionally carry, per entry, the following keys:
.PP
.RS
.nf
entry[N].filename         member file name inside the archive
entry[N].class            "regular" or "flash"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The \fBget\fP options request a single metadata value: \fB\-n\fP the on-calc
variable name, \fB\-f\fP the on-calc folder name (TI-68k), \fB\-t\fP the
variable type as an integer, \fB\-h\fP the file magic number, \fB\-V\fP the
file version (TI-Z80, TI-eZ80), \fB\-c\fP the file comment, \fB\-a\fP and
\fB\-l\fP the archived and locked information, \fB\-x\fP the stored and
computed data checksums. The requested value is printed on stdout.
.PP
The \fBset\fP options set the corresponding fields, and may be combined:
\fB\-n NAME\fP the on-calc variable name, \fB\-f NAME\fP the on-calc folder
name (TI-68k), \fB\-t TYPE\fP the variable type as an integer (which must be
valid for the calculator model), \fB\-h MAGIC\fP the file magic number (which
must match the calculator model), \fB\-V VERSION\fP the file version (0-255,
TI-Z80, TI-eZ80), \fB\-c COMMENT\fP the file comment (strftime format string,
expanded when it contains a '%'), \fB\-\-raw\-comment=COMMENT\fP the file
comment set literally (without strftime expansion; the last of \fB\-c\fP and
\fB\-\-raw\-comment\fP given wins), \fB\-a 0/1\fP the archived information
(archived implies locked; TI-83+/84+ and TI-68k files can carry it),
\fB\-l 0/1\fP the locked information (TI-68k), \fB\-x VALUE|fix\fP the stored
data checksum, set to the given value (regular TI-8x and backup files, which
store it in the last 2 bytes of the file) or recomputed (\fBfix\fP). Setting
a metadata field which cannot be stored by the file format, or which is
read-only, is an error, as are invalid values; the file is rewritten only
when every requested change is valid.
.PP
Entries are numbered starting at 1, the regular entries first, then the flash
entries. If the input file is \fB\-\fP, or has an
unknown extension, or cannot be parsed, an error message is printed on stderr
and the exit status is 3.

.TP
.B group -m MODE [OPTIONS] [FILE ...]
Work on tigroup files (ZIP archives of TI files, as produced by TI-Connect).
The MODE is one of \fBlist\fP, \fBcreate\fP, \fBextract\fP and \fBappend\fP.
All options must precede the file name(s).
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fBlist\fP: list the entries of a single tigroup file on stdout, under the
form of a small table (model, comment, entry count, then the size, class and
member file name of every entry, with the on-calc variable name and type for
regular entries)
.IP \(bu
\fBcreate\fP: create a new tigroup file from a list of input files, which can
be single, group, flash or tigroup files, the latter being merged entry by
entry. The \fB\-t\fP option is mandatory, and only
the "tigroup" type is supported for now. The \fB\-o OUTFILE\fP option is
mandatory.
.IP \(bu
\fBextract\fP: extract the entries of a single tigroup file into the
directory given by the \fB\-d PATH\fP option (default: the current
directory), each entry being written under its member file name. Member names
containing a directory separator, or equal to "." or "..", are rejected.
.IP \(bu
\fBappend\fP: append a list of input files (single, group, flash or tigroup
files) to an existing tigroup file,
writing the result to the \fB\-o OUTFILE\fP file if given, and rewriting the
input tigroup file otherwise. Appending an entry whose member file name
already exists in the tigroup is an error. If the input tigroup file does not
exist, a new tigroup file is created from the given files, under the
\fB\-o OUTFILE\fP file if given and under the (nonexistent) input file
otherwise.
.RE
.PP
.RS
.IP \(bu
\fB\-m MODE\fP: the mode (required)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-t TYPE\fP: for create, the type of file to create, only "tigroup" is
supported (required)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-o OUTFILE\fP: for create, the tigroup file to create (required); for
append, the output file (default: the input tigroup file)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-d PATH\fP: for extract, the destination directory (default: current
directory)
.IP \(bu
\fB\-v\fP: be verbose
.RE

.SH EXAMPLES
Dump a file supposed to be suitable for TI calculators as a C hex array to an output file:

.TP
tifileutil dump -f chexarray romdump_9x/romdump.89z -o rom89.h -n romDump89 -s romDumpSize89 -c 8 -m 64

.TP
Create TI file header and footer for a raw binary:

.TP
tifileutil wrap \-n 'ROMDUMP' dump83p.bin \-p \-o dump83p.8xp

.TP
Check the checksum of a file, and fix it if necessary:

.TP
tifileutil checksum dump83p.8xp

.TP
tifileutil checksum \-f \-o dump83p_fixed.8xp dump83p.8xp

.TP
Extract the raw binary content of a variable, stripping the header and trailing checksum:

.TP
tifileutil unwrap dump83p.8xp \-o dump83p.bin

.TP
Wrap data as a complex variable, e.g. a complex number for the TI-83+ family
or a complex exact fraction/radical/pi for the TI-83PCE/TI-84PCE:

.TP
tifileutil wrap \-n 'Z' value.bin \-C \-t 8xn \-o z.8xc

.TP
Squish an assembly program stored as hexadecimal text in a TI-83 program, and
unsquish it back:

.TP
tifileutil squish code.83p \-o code.83p

.TP
tifileutil unsquish code.83p \-o code.83p

.TP
Turn a TI-68k assembly program into a TI-BASIC Exec string:

.TP
tifileutil unsquish program.89z

.TP
Pack a Python script into a TI-83PCE/TI-84+CE Python AppVar, and extract it back:

.TP
tifileutil wrap \-P \-n 'HELLO' \-t 8xv hello.py \-o hello.8xv

.TP
tifileutil unwrap \-d hello.8xv \-o hello.py

